    "vertical_acceleration_g": 0.079,
    "note": "The crest, on the boundary between the two halves of Il Banco. Half-length 51.18 m against 63.86 m of shelf either side, so it clears T1's exit tangent by 12.68 m and T2's turn-in by 12.68 m; T2's braking zone is 9.5 m, so it clears the braking point by 3.2 m. 1.48x forward and 1.62x reverse. This is the tightest fit on the circuit and it is what caps the elevation range."
   },
   {
    "at_m": 550.731737,
    "profile": "concave",
    "K": 20.0,
    "radius_m": 400.0,
    "length_m": 36.8,
    "grade_in_pct": -4.6,
    "grade_out_pct": 4.6,
    "speed_forward_kmh": 52.0,
    "speed_reverse_kmh": 52.0,
    "vertical_acceleration_g": 0.053,
    "note": "The compression, and the only vertical curve on the circuit inside a corner arc. It is a deliberate art 7.2 exception and is argued as one rather than reported as compliant: a 9.20% grade break is the whole descent-to-climb transition and there is nowhere else on the lap it fits. At the hairpin it is taken at 52.0 km/h in both directions, needs 135.06 m of radius, gets 400 m (2.96x) and occupies 36.80 m inside a 47.12 m arc - 5.16 m clear of each tangent point. It is CONCAVE, so it adds 0.053 g of load at the slowest point on the circuit rather than removing it, which is the benign direction. Terrain put a CONVEX 8.51% break inside its tightest corner and unloaded the driven axle by 0.118 g at the apex; this does the opposite."
   },
   {
    "at_m": 675.804093,
    "profile": "convex",
